WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.417
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Welcome to "Get

00:00:01.417 --> 00:00:03.570
Cooking in Cloud," where
we share the best recipes

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:05.430
to apply in your cloud kitchen.

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:06.210
I am Priyanka.

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.850
And in this episode,
we'll talk about a hot DR

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:12.282
pattern for applications that
are deployed on Google Cloud.

00:00:12.282 --> 00:00:15.726
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:24.540
We met Main Street Art
in the previous recipe.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:26.640
But now, they've
gotten so popular

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.400
that they now have a
huge and global customer

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.720
base, which requires them
to be online all the time.

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:35.790
So they are setting up a
hot DR pattern with next

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:37.150
to no downtime.

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:40.553
Now, Main Street Art has
a unique advantage here.

00:00:40.553 --> 00:00:42.720
Since they're deploying
their production application

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:45.030
on Google Cloud, they
can take full advantage

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:47.100
of the built-in HA features.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:48.760
Here's how they
would set this up.

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:50.790
They would have to
create a VPC network

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:52.380
and then create a
custom image that's

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:54.780
configured with the
application service,

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:57.060
use that image to create
an instance template,

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:59.490
using this instance template
to configure a regionally

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:00.990
managed instance group.

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:02.740
This is to ensure resilience.

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:04.860
If the whole zone
was to go down,

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:08.070
the instance will still be
available in other regions

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:10.030
to keep the application running.

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:12.540
Make sure the HTTP and
instance health checks

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:14.750
are configured at the
managed instance groups

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:17.880
to verify that the services
and instances are properly

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:19.290
running within a group.

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:22.470
This ensures that if a
service or an instance fails,

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:25.530
the group automatically
recreates that instance.

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:28.040
Configure load balancing
using the managed instance

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:30.510
group that spans across zones.

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:33.360
Create Cloud SQL in
one zone as a master

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.790
and enable a replica
in another zone.

00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:39.420
Doing this means that in case
the master becomes unavailable,

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:42.520
they can promote the replica
to become the master,

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:45.270
and then finally configure
the Cloud DNS to point

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:47.010
to the primary application.

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:49.950
As the application
has built-in HA,

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:52.380
if a part of the
architecture fails,

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:54.330
it basically heals itself.

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:57.480
And Main Street Art
team has to do nothing

00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:59.670
to ensure their
application continues

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:02.080
to be available for
their customers.

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:04.860
If you would like to learn more
about building scalable web

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:07.980
apps on Google Cloud, check
out the previous episodes.

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:09.430
Well, there you have it.

00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:12.150
If you are deploying an
application on Google Cloud

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:16.560
and have to meet those really
small RTO and RPO values,

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:19.740
then use some of the built-in
Google Cloud HA features.

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:21.960
That's all for today on
"Get Cooking in Cloud."

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:25.470
Here's hoping you're whipping
up your own DR strategy.

